Usages:
  single_choke_point_send: |
    All outgoing messages, regardless of type (text, receipt, sync message, group update),
    are funneled through one send method taking an already-prepared outgoing message value.
    Preparation (recipient resolution, attachment staging) happens before this cell is
    involved; this cell's only responsibility is turning a prepared message into encrypted,
    per-device ciphertext and getting it to the server.
  pipeline_suspension: |
    Message fetching and processing can be suspended for specific named reasons (app waking
    from a notification-service extension, registration/provisioning in progress, an
    in-progress change-number operation, device linking) without the suspending code needing
    to know which concrete pipeline stages exist; each stage registers itself and reacts to
    suspend/resume uniformly.

Annotations: |
  This cell is the core outgoing/incoming message pipeline: a single send choke point, a
  buffering/dispatch layer for incoming envelopes, and a suspension mechanism shared by both
  directions. Its public contract is intentionally narrow — most of its internal wiring
  (session/account/thread state) is private construction detail, not part of this contract.
  Use `single_choke_point_send` for `MessageSender`.
  Use `pipeline_suspension` for `MessagePipelineSupervisor`.

---

"MessageSender()":
  location: MessageSender.swift
  annotations: |
    Protocol exposing the single entry point for sending an already-prepared outgoing message.

    Use `single_choke_point_send` for why this is a single method rather than per-message-type
    methods.
  methods:
    "sendMessage(preparedOutgoingMessage: String) -> result: String": |
      Encrypts the prepared message for each of the recipient's devices and sends it to the
      server, returning the outcome.

      `preparedOutgoingMessage`: the message to send, already resolved and staged for sending

      Algorithm:
      1. Build per-device ciphertext, establishing a Signal Protocol session first if none exists.
      2. Choose sealed-sender or identified sending per the message/recipient's requirements.
      3. Dispatch the resulting device messages to the server and classify the response.

"MessageProcessor()":
  location: MessageProcessor.swift
  annotations: |
    Buffers raw fetched envelope data and asynchronously batches it through decryption and
    dispatch to the receiver layer.
  methods:
    "enqueueReceivedEnvelopeData(envelopeData: Data, serverDeliveryTimestamp: Int)": |
      Adds a freshly fetched, still-encrypted envelope to the processing queue.
    "waitForFetchingAndProcessing()": |
      Suspends the caller until every currently enqueued envelope has been fetched and
      processed.

"MessagePipelineSupervisor()":
  location: MessagePipelineSupervisor.swift
  annotations: |
    Thread-safe coordination point letting any subsystem pause and resume every registered
    message-processing stage without knowing which stages exist.

    Use `pipeline_suspension` for the overall coordination algorithm.
  methods:
    "suspendMessageProcessing(reason: String)": |
      Pauses every registered pipeline stage until the matching resume call is made.
    "unsuspendMessageProcessing(reason: String)": |
      Resumes pipeline stages previously suspended for `reason`.
    "register(pipelineStage: String)": |
      Registers a stage to receive suspend/resume notifications.

"MessageReceiver()":
  location: MessageReceiver.swift
  annotations: |
    Consumes a decrypted envelope and routes its content to the appropriate handler
    (data message, sync message, call message, typing indicator, or receipt).
  methods:
    "processEnvelope(envelope: String, plaintextData: Data?, serverDeliveryTimestamp: Int, tx: DBWriteTransaction)": |
      Dispatches one decrypted envelope's content to the handler appropriate for its type,
      persisting whatever state that entails (e.g. inserting a resulting interaction).

